# Citation metadata, in the Citation File Format (https://citation-file-format.org).
# GitHub reads this and shows a "Cite this repository" button.  Zenodo reads
# .zenodo.json instead when minting a DOI from a release, and ignores this file
# while that one exists; the test suite holds the fields the two share equal.
#
####   On each tagged release, bump `version` and `date-released` to match,
####   and in .zenodo.json bump `version`, the version in `title`, and the tag
####   in the GitHub tree link.  The version here should equal the one a run
####   log reports, or the citation names software that did not produce the
####   result.  The test suite holds all four equal to shelterprep/version.py.
####   `version` is bumped when the work is done rather than when the tag
####   lands, so `date-released` runs ahead of the tag until CHANGELOG.md's
####   Unreleased section is given its number; check it at tag time.
#
####   For `doi:`, use Zenodo's CONCEPT DOI -- the version-independent one that
####   always resolves to the newest release -- not the DOI of a single release.
####   A release's own DOI does not exist until that release is archived, by
####   which point this file is already inside it, so it could only ever name
####   the release before.  The concept DOI is stable, and `version` above is
####   what says which release was actually run.
#
####   Enable the repository in Zenodo BEFORE tagging.  Zenodo archives
####   releases made after it is switched on and does not reach back for
####   earlier ones.
#
####   AFTER publishing a release, check the Zenodo record against
####   .zenodo.json: title `ShelterDataPrep (Version X.Y.Z)`, version `X.Y.Z`
####   without a `v`, the three related works, and the two references.  Without
####   that file Zenodo takes the version from the tag name, and a related work
####   or reference entered on one version's record does not carry to the
####   next.  A field name Zenodo does not know is dropped without notice, so
####   check in the Zenodo interface at the time.  Zenodo fails the archive on a
####   .zenodo.json it cannot read; the test suite checks that the file parses
####   and uses only the field and relation names Zenodo accepts.
#
####   A related work or reference is added, changed, or removed in
####   .zenodo.json before tagging, not on the Zenodo record: Zenodo rebuilds
####   each version's record from that file, so an edit made on the record is
####   lost at the next release.
#
####   The two "Is supplemented by" related works in .zenodo.json name the
####   deposits' CONCEPT DOIs, 10.5281/zenodo.22051367 (the prepared files)
####   and 10.5281/zenodo.22051090 (the raw extracts).  A related work says
####   which deposits belong with the software, not which bytes a run read, so
####   it follows each deposit to its newest version and stays the same across
####   releases.  The `references` below are the opposite case and name
####   version DOIs, for the reason given there.
#
####   .zenodo.json names no license, so Zenodo takes the record's from the
####   repository's LICENSE file.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it as below.  If you use the Orange
  County and Los Angeles County data that the shipped configs cover, also cite
  the deposit it comes from: 10.5281/zenodo.22051091 for the raw extracts, or
  10.5281/zenodo.22051368 if you took the prepared files rather than preparing
  them yourself.
title: "ShelterDataPrep"
abstract: >-
  Config-driven preparation of animal shelter intake and outcome extracts for
  length-of-stay analysis.  A run is one YAML settings file, and produces the
  prepared data alongside a processing ledger that records every row removed or
  changed, a value-level breakdown of each filter and mapping, descriptive
  statistics of the finished set, and a provenance log.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
    given-names: "Michael Loizos"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
version: "0.5.0"
date-released: "2026-10-05"
license: MIT
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051338"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Shelter-Data-Analysis/ShelterDataPrep"
keywords:
  - animal shelter
  - length of stay
  - data preparation
  - reproducible research

# The two deposits this software sits between: the extracts the shipped
# configs read, and the files they produce.  Both are cited by their VERSION
# DOI rather than their concept DOI -- the opposite of the rule for `doi:`
# above, and for the same underlying reason.  A run log pins a file by digest,
# so only a version DOI is guaranteed to still hold the bytes that produced a
# result.  If a deposit's own title or author list changes, change it here
# too: a citation that does not match the record is not a citation.
references:
  - type: data
    title: >-
      Raw animal-level data from government-operated animal shelters in Orange
      County and Los Angeles County, California
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051091"
    version: "1"
    year: 2026
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      The six extracts read by every shipped config except example_tiny.yaml.
      A run over your own shelter's extract has nothing to do with this
      deposit and should not cite it.
  - type: data
    title: >-
      Processed animal-level data, with descriptive statistics, from
      government-operated animal shelters in Orange County and Los Angeles
      County, California
    authors:
      - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
        given-names: "Michael Loizos"
        orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
    doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22051368"
    year: 2026
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      What the shipped configs produce from the extracts above, under version
      0.3.0, with each prepared file's statistics table, run log, and settings
      file.  Cite this instead of rerunning if you used those files as they
      are.
